WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES

  The console evidence for probe query two: a DATAFLOW route over the SAME
  handler/sink pair that query one reaches by call graph. It is one of the four
  heap-bound JVM invocations this run records separately - the jimple2cpg
  frontend build, the importCpg verification load, the Stage 3 Joern runner, and
  this probe - and, with query one, one of the two whose subject is a question
  about Joern rather than a question about Spark.

  It carries, in execution order: the preconditions that had to hold before the
  query was allowed to load the graph, the target surface the query addresses
  measured in the pinned tree rather than assumed, the exact command with its
  JDK major version and the heap it actually received recorded as three separate
  fields, what the invocation reported, the duplicate-formulation verdict against
  query one reasoned on substance, the spurious-route definition with its
  selectors quoted verbatim, the cross-checks against the two result files, and
  the terminal transcript verbatim.

  The <nn>-<slug> in this file's name matches the query source exactly:
  queries/joern/02-dataflow-unguarded-driver-launch.sc.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is OBSERVATIONAL. It is not a finding, and nothing in
  it is an assessment of Spark, of any Spark component or of any Spark
  configuration. It judges no finding - not real, not important, not a false
  positive, not a duplicate of another tool's - and it makes no comparison
  between tools, which is why no other scanner is named anywhere in it. It
  contributes NO row to oss-scan-results/findings.json and writes nothing into
  harness/artifacts/raw/. Joern appears twice in this run by design: once as one
  of the nine scanned runners, and once as the subject of this probe. Nothing
  here is added to, merged with or compared against that runner's output, and no
  figure in this file is arithmetic over the two.

  AND IT IS NEVER SUMMED WITH QUERY ONE. Two queries over one pair invite the
  reader to add their returns together, which would double-count the same route
  and inflate the capability claim. Their results are reported side by side, as
  distinct, and no arithmetic anywhere in this file combines them.

