  THE HALT-CLASS FINDING, stated plainly. The bytes on disk are 548118435 with
  sha256 f8c71562...d22c53. The pair harness/artifacts/logs/cpg-frontend.log
  records at write time is 541255894 with sha256 26d327cc...fcffc. The
  difference is +6862541 bytes and a different digest: not a truncation, not a
  partial write, and not a link pointing at the wrong place - STEP 2 showed all
  three names resolve to one inode. These are simply different bytes, and AAP
  0.9.2 makes that a halt at any later load. The query itself reached the same
  verdict independently, inside its own process, and stopped: APPENDIX B lines
  21-29, where its own two "matches" lines both read NO, and APPENDIX A.2, where
  the exception it raised on them is printed in full.

STEP 4 - WHERE THE DIFFERENCE CAME FROM, established without this query's help
  A halt says the load must not happen. It does not say why the bytes changed,
  and leaving that unexplained would invite the two worst readings: that the
  graph was corrupted, or that some stage of this run quietly rewrote it. The
  four measurements below rule out both, and they come from files this query
  neither writes nor reads.

  The first shows the live file's own timestamps. The second is the write-time
  identity recorded by the provisioning invocation that produced THESE bytes -
  a record independent of the in-repo one, and it agrees with STEP 2 and STEP 3
  exactly. The third is that same invocation's verification of the graph it
  built, which is where the method, type-declaration and file counts for the
  bytes now on disk come from. The fourth confirms the in-repo record never
  mentions the live byte size anywhere in its 37846 lines, so the two records
  describe two graphs rather than one graph inconsistently.

